{#
    The bar under a sell-side document's line table: a [line type] select plus an Add Line control.
    Include it rather than copying it.

    It does not add PRODUCT rows. It adds a CHARGE row — shipping, tax or a one-off fee — which the
    document posts back as charge_lines[] and SalesDocumentChargeLines turns into that document's
    shipping lines, manual tax lines and manual fee lines. On the order and the quote it is the only
    way to set shipping at all: the type=shipping rows ARE the document's shipping, so there is no
    second mechanism left to double-count.

    Three templates carried it — admin/order/form.html.twig, admin/estimate/form.html.twig and
    admin/invoice/create_standalone.html.twig — and the literal <option> lines were identical in all
    three, which is the whole vocabulary of the control. The invoice's copy had already drifted: it
    dropped the carrier's delivery time from the shipping options its own controller computes. One
    definition cannot drift.

    "Custom Shipping" and "Empty Shipping Line" are two options for the same thing — a shipping row
    with no fixed method — one with a preset label an admin cannot change, one free-text. Order and
    the quote both pass `categoryFilter` (#669 for the order, extended to the quote per the sell-side
    parity ruling — a quote's charge picker has no justified reason to differ from the order's), so
    both drop the preset-label one and relabel the free-text one "Custom Shipping (Type anything)":
    one way to add an unnamed shipping charge, not two that only differ in whether the label can be
    edited. The standalone invoice does not pass it yet and keeps both, unchanged.

    An include and not a macro, for the reason product_field.html.twig and region_select.html.twig
    are includes: three callers with three different parameter sets, one of which passes no JS hook
    at all, and `with_context = false` makes every one of them state what it wants in full.

    Parameters
    ----------
    shippingOptions     required  the carrier options this document prices against — id, label,
                                  amount, deliveryDays, taxClass. Pass [] for none.
    submitLabel         required  the text on the submit that adds a row server-side. Plain text;
                                  write '&' as '&', it is escaped here.
    submitClass         required  that submit's class. The order and the quote use
                                  'button primary no-js-inline' because their JS button below does
                                  the same job when scripting is on, so the submit is hidden then;
                                  a document with no line JS passes a class that is always visible.
    hook                optional  'order' or 'estimate' — the document app.js keys its per-form
                                  handlers off, as .js-<hook>-charge-type and .js-<hook>-bottom-add.
                                  Pass '' (or omit) for a document with no line JS: the select then
                                  carries no hook class and the JS "Add Line" button is not rendered
                                  at all, because a type="button" nothing is bound to is a dead
                                  control and this page's submit is the real one.
    shippingOptionsUrl  optional  where app.js re-fetches carrier options when the document changes
                                  under it. Only the order has an endpoint for this.
    sentinel            optional  defaults to false. Emits the charge_lines_present hidden input.
    categoryFilter       optional  defaults to false. Adds two JS-only controls above the full
                                  choices select — a Tax/Fee/Shipping category picker that filters
                                  it, and a Main Line/Before Tax/After Tax placement picker that
                                  appears once a fee is chosen. Both are `.js-only`, so a no-JS admin
                                  never sees them and keeps using the full select exactly as before.
                                  Passed by the order (#669) and the quote (sell-side parity); left
                                  false for the standalone invoice, which has no line JS at all.

    ## charge_lines_present, and why the standalone invoice does not carry it

    A document that is REBUILT from what its form posts cannot tell "the admin removed every charge
    row" from "this post was never shown the charge rows" — both arrive as no charge_lines[] — and
    guessing wrong wipes a stored document's shipping, tax adjustments and manual fees on the first
    innocent save. So the forms that edit a stored document render this marker beside the control
    that shows the rows, and only a post carrying it is allowed to clear charges. OrderController,
    EstimateController and ProcurementBundle's PurchaseOrderController all read it that way.

    The standalone invoice create page passes sentinel = false, deliberately. It is a CREATE screen
    with no stored document behind it: InvoiceController::create() builds the charge rows from the
    post and mints the invoice in the same request, so there is nothing to preserve and no ambiguity
    to resolve. InvoiceController does not read `charge_lines_present` anywhere, so emitting it would
    be markup that no code has an opinion about — and adding an input on the theory that three out of
    four is a rule is how a convention becomes cargo. If that page ever grows an edit mode, the
    marker comes with it.
#}
<div class="order-add-line-bar">
    {% if categoryFilter|default(false) and hook|default('') %}
        {# JS-only (#669): a quick Shipping/Tax/Fee filter in front of the full select below, which
           it filters rather than replaces — the full select is what actually posts, with or without
           this control's help, so hiding some of its <option>s here changes nothing about what a
           no-JS admin sees or can choose. #}
        <select class="js-{{ hook }}-charge-category js-only" aria-label="Filter line type by category">
            <option value="">All types</option>
            <option value="shipping">Shipping</option>
            <option value="tax">Tax</option>
            <option value="fee">Fee</option>
        </select>
    {% endif %}
    {# `name` so the chosen type actually posts without JS. The JS path never reads it —
       .js-<hook>-bottom-add takes the value straight off this element and then blanks it — so a JS
       save always posts an empty one, and only the submit below asks the server to act on it. #}
    <select{% if hook|default('') %} class="js-{{ hook }}-charge-type"{% endif %} name="charge_line_type" aria-label="Select line type to add"{% if shippingOptionsUrl|default('') %} data-shipping-options-url="{{ shippingOptionsUrl }}"{% endif %}>
        <option value=""></option>
        {% for opt in shippingOptions|default([]) %}
            {# The delivery time is part of what an admin is choosing between — "$15.00" and
               "$30.84" is not a choice until it reads "(5 days)" and "(2 days)". Every one of these
               resolvers computes deliveryDays and every controller passes it through; the invoice's
               own copy of this line was the one that dropped it on the floor. #}
            <option value="shipping-method:{{ opt.label }}|{{ opt.amount }}">{{ opt.label }}{% if opt.deliveryDays %} ({{ opt.deliveryDays }} day{{ opt.deliveryDays != 1 ? 's' : '' }}){% endif %} — ${{ opt.amount|amount }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
        {% if not categoryFilter|default(false) %}
            <option value="shipping:Custom Shipping">Custom Shipping</option>
        {% endif %}
        <option value="tax:Custom GST">Custom GST</option>
        <option value="tax:Custom PST">Custom PST</option>
        <option value="tax:Custom HST">Custom HST</option>
        {# #669: this used to be a second, free-text way to add an unnamed shipping charge,
           alongside "Custom Shipping" above with a fixed, uneditable label. Wherever the fixed one
           is gone (see `categoryFilter` above — the order and the quote), this is the only way, so
           it says so; the standalone invoice, with no `categoryFilter`, keeps the original wording
           and both options. #}
        <option value="empty-shipping:">{{ categoryFilter|default(false) ? 'Custom Shipping (Type anything)' : 'Empty Shipping Line (Type Anything)' }}</option>
        {# Rewording under `categoryFilter` (#669): "Empty ... Line" read like a blank placeholder
           rather than a free-text charge an admin types a name into. The standalone invoice keeps
           the original wording. #}
        <option value="empty-tax:">{{ categoryFilter|default(false) ? 'Custom Tax (Type Anything)' : 'Empty Tax Line (Type Anything)' }}</option>
        {# The one-off charge that is neither tax nor shipping and isn't worth a reusable Fee
           definition. It becomes a manual fee line in the fee_lines snapshot. #}
        <option value="empty-fee:">{{ categoryFilter|default(false) ? 'Custom Fee (Type Anything)' : 'Empty Fee Line (Type Anything)' }}</option>
    </select>
    {% if categoryFilter|default(false) and hook|default('') %}
        {# JS-only (#669): where a fee's total lands — as part of the line-item subtotal, or as a
           flat addition before/after tax is applied — used to live on the row itself, edited any
           time after the row existed. Nothing ever moved the row to match a changed choice, so
           picking "After Tax" on an existing row did not do what it said. Asked once, here, before
           the row exists, there is nothing left to get out of sync. #}
        <select class="js-{{ hook }}-charge-placement-choice js-only" aria-label="Fee placement" hidden>
            <option value="main_line">Main Line</option>
            <option value="before_tax_line">Before Tax</option>
            <option value="after_tax_line">After Tax</option>
        </select>
    {% endif %}
    {% if hook|default('') %}
        <button class="button primary js-{{ hook }}-bottom-add" type="button">Add Line</button>
    {% endif %}
    {# The real control. On a form whose JS button above is a type="button" — and therefore a dead
       control without scripting — this is the no-JS twin, hidden by .no-js-inline whenever the JS
       one works: it appends a charge row of the selected type, saves and recalculates in one POST,
       landing back on the form with the new row's inputs on it. On a form with no JS button it is
       simply the button, always visible. #}
    <button class="{{ submitClass }}" type="submit" name="add_charge_line" value="1">{{ submitLabel }}</button>
</div>
{% if sentinel|default(false) %}
    {# See the note above: only a form that REBUILDS a stored document needs to say that this post
       had the charge rows in front of it. #}
    <input type="hidden" name="charge_lines_present" value="1">
{% endif %}
